## The short version

Assemble **eight artifacts of your own real project communication**, with links: **2 bug reports · 1 feature request · 1 PR description · 2 review comments · 2 responses to feedback.** Each with a one-or-two-sentence note on why you chose it. Graded on **quality, not completion** — eight thoughtful artifacts, not eight boxes checked.

---

## Why this assignment exists

Here's the open secret of software engineering: most of what a working engineer *writes* is not code. It's bug reports, PR descriptions, review comments, issue threads, and replies to feedback — thousands of small texts that collectively determine whether a team (or an open-source community) functions. This writing is engineering: a reproducible bug report saves a maintainer an hour; a clear PR description makes review possible; a kind, precise review comment improves both the code and the colleague. And in open source, this writing *is your public face* — it's genuinely the thing strangers know you by.

The portfolio exists to make you treat this writing as work worth doing well — and worth *curating*. By collecting your best instead of your most recent, you're forced to reread your own communication with an editor's eye, which is how the standard becomes internal. The eighth-grade version of this skill is "wrote it down"; the professional version is *precise, complete, actionable, kind* — and the gap between them is one of the most career-shaping gaps there is. Notice, too, that two of the eight artifacts are **responses to feedback**: communication is two-way, and how you receive critique is as visible, and as reputation-defining, as how you give it.

---

## What to produce

A single document (Markdown or PDF) with **eight linked artifacts**, each with a 1–2 sentence note on why it represents your best work:

| # | Artifact | The bar |
|---|---|---|
| 2× | **Bug reports** | The anatomy from Session 15: one failure, a title that names it, numbered steps to reproduce, expected vs. actual, environment, evidence. |
| 1× | **Feature request** | The need, the user, the current workaround — your product-definition thinking in miniature, not "add X pls." |
| 1× | **PR description** | Problem · approach · what to look at first; a reviewer could start cold. |
| 2× | **Review comments** | Substantive, specific, kind; on the code, not the coder; a question that improves the work counts. |
| 2× | **Responses to feedback** | You engaging critique — fixing, or explaining a reasoned disagreement — without defensiveness. |

Legitimate sources: your project's repository, the Session 14 git lab, the issues your cold tester filed against your setup guide (and your replies), issues or reviews you've written on a classmate's project, and any real open-source interaction you've had this semester. Everything must be **yours** and **from this course period.**

---

## Requirements & constraints

- **Quality over quantity is literal:** the counts are a floor for coverage, but one thoughtful bug report outweighs six rote ones — the rubric grades the writing, not the tally.
- **Links must resolve** (public repo or instructor-accessible). Screenshots as backup if a thread might move.
- **Choose deliberately.** The 1–2 sentence "why I chose this" notes are read; "it was my most recent one" is not curation.
- **Start collecting now, not the night before it's due.** The good artifacts are the ones you wrote carefully in the moment — retroactive polish shows.

